WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
[Music]

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.000
Welcome to Introduction to Web Accessibility.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.500
In this lesson, we'll explore why accessibility matters
and how to build inclusive web experiences.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.000
Over one billion people worldwide live with some form of disability.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.500
Web accessibility, often abbreviated as a11y,
ensures everyone can use the web.

6
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.000
The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, or WCAG,
provide the international standard.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
WCAG is organized around four principles:
Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, and Robust.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.500
Let's start with Perceivable.
All content must be presentable in ways users can perceive.

9
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.000
One of the most important practices is providing
alternative text for images.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.500
Alt text describes the content and function of an image
for users who cannot see it.

11
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.000
[Screen reader speaking]
"Image: A student using a screen reader to navigate a website."

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.500
That was a screen reader reading aloud the alt text
we provided for that image.

13
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.000
Next, let's talk about semantic HTML.
Using the correct HTML elements conveys meaning to assistive technology.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.500
For example, use heading tags like h1 through h6
to create a logical document outline.

15
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.000
Use nav for navigation, main for primary content,
and aside for supplementary content.

16
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.500
[Applause]
Great question from the chat about color contrast.

17
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.000
WCAG requires a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1
for normal text, and 3 to 1 for large text.

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.500
Now let's discuss keyboard navigation.
Every interactive element must be reachable and operable with a keyboard.

19
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.000
Focus indicators show users where they are on the page.
Never remove the default focus outline without providing an alternative.

20
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
ARIA, or Accessible Rich Internet Applications,
extends HTML when native semantics are not enough.

21
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000
But remember the first rule of ARIA:
do not use ARIA if a native HTML element can do the job.

22
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Captions and transcripts are essential for video content.
They help users who are deaf or hard of hearing.

23
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Audio descriptions narrate visual information
for users who are blind or have low vision.

24
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
[Screen reader speaking]
"Button: Submit form."
That is how a screen reader announces a properly labeled button.

25
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Forms need associated labels.
Every input must have a corresponding label element.

26
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Error messages should be clear, specific,
and programmatically associated with the relevant field.

27
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Testing is crucial. Use tools like Lighthouse, axe,
and manual screen reader testing.

28
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
In Ethiopia, accessibility is especially important
for reaching learners in diverse communities.

29
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Digital Bridge aims to make ALX course content
accessible to all Ethiopian learners, regardless of ability.

30
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Let's recap: use semantic HTML, provide alt text,
ensure keyboard access, and test with real users.

31
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Accessibility is not a feature — it is a fundamental right.
Building inclusively benefits everyone.

32
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.000
[Applause]
Thank you for joining this session.

33
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
In our next lesson, we will build an accessible
navigation component from scratch.

34
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Keep practicing, and remember:
the web is for everyone.

35
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.000
[Music]
